kerbridge (@VERSION@) unstable; urgency=@URGENCY@

  The audit logs and the per-daemon state directories have moved. Each daemon
  now writes its record to /var/log/kerbridge/<daemon>/ and its state to
  /var/lib/kerbridge/<daemon>/ -- one directory per daemon, each owned by that
  daemon. The old compiled-in default was /var/lib/kerbridge-audit/, which was
  a Docker-ism: /var/log is where the FHS puts logs, and auditd is the
  precedent for audit records specifically. One directory per daemon is what
  stops a compromised broker from rewriting the issuer's append-only record,
  and it is what leaves logrotate able to create the successor file.

  If you never stated audit_log_file in your configuration set, there is
  nothing to do: templates have been sparse since 0.9.0, so a set that does not
  name the option simply takes the new default. If you did state the old path
  explicitly, your value is still honoured and still used -- change it
  yourself, or run "kbconfig upgrade", which replays the recorded change.

  Nothing at the old path is moved or deleted, on installation or on purge. An
  audit record is not the packaging system's to remove.

 -- @MAINTAINER@  @DATE@
